Answer:
// is the integer division operator.

In Python 3 the ordinary / division operator returns floating point values even if both operands are integers, so a different operator is needed for integer division. This is different from Python 2 where / performed integer division if both operands where integers and floating point division if at least one of the operands was a floating point value.

The // operator was first introduced for forward-compatibility in Python 2.2 when it was decided that Python 3 should have this new ability. Together with the ability to enable the Python 3 behaviour via from __future__ import division (also introduced in Python 2.2), this enables you to write Python 3-compatible code in Python 2.

